# LinkedIn Dual-Credential Architecture

## Two Apps, Two Token Rows

| App | Purpose | Env Vars | Platform Token Key | Scopes |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Original app | Publishing posts | `LINKEDIN_CLIENT_ID` / `LINKEDIN_CLIENT_SECRET` | `linkedin` | `w_member_social` |
| Sovereign Sanctuary Engine 1.0 | Comments, reactions | `LINKEDIN_COMMUNITY_CLIENT_ID` / `LINKEDIN_COMMUNITY_CLIENT_SECRET` | `linkedin_community` | `w_member_social`, `r_member_social` (Community Management API) |

LinkedIn requires the Community Management API to be the **only product** on an app to grant comment read/write access. The original app has "Share on LinkedIn" which conflicts, so a second app is needed.

## Adapter Architecture

- `LinkedInAdapter` (platform=`linkedin`) — Main adapter for publishing. On `authenticate()`, also loads the community token from the `linkedin_community` row in `skyeye_platform_tokens`.
- `LinkedInCommunityAdapter` (platform=`linkedin_community`) — Subclass used solely for OAuth authorization of the community app. Stores its token under `linkedin_community`.
- `_social_actions_token` property — Returns community token if available, falls back to main token. Used by `get_comments()`, `reply_to_comment()`, `get_post_reactions()`, `delete_comment()`.

## OAuth Flow

- **Posting app**: `GET /api/skyeye/platforms/linkedin/connect`
- **Community app**: `GET /api/skyeye/platforms/linkedin_community/connect`
- Both use the same generic callback handler at `/api/skyeye/platforms/{platform}/callback`
- Redirect URI for community app: `https://api.sovereignsanctuary.net/api/skyeye/platforms/linkedin_community/callback`

## Community App Credentials

- Client ID: `86xz2vub2aiqr9`
- App name: "Sovereign Sanctuary Engine 1.0"
- Created: Feb 24, 2026
- Requires Community Management API product approval on LinkedIn Developer Portal

## When Modifying

- Never merge the two apps into one — LinkedIn's product rules prevent it
- If adding new socialActions endpoints, use `self._social_actions_token` (not `self._access_token`)
- If the community token is missing, socialActions calls fall back to the posting token (which returns 403 — handled gracefully with `needs_manual_post` flag)
